11-05 | Alex：\"改\"。只改读法里那一句：原句\"写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"→ 新句\"只写算出它的那几行，或换了名字的同一个量；更大的行、旁边的行都不写\"（任务 790 → 889 字符，理由：抽象说法换成闭合两类加边界）。提示词审计、Codex 只读审 1 轮（1 条严重：发送失败会被当成白卷，已改成\"不完整、不下结论\"）；先在 v19、v20、v10、v12 上试发 24 次（1.6 美分） | 试发过：v19、v20 三次都白卷，v10 3/3；v12 2/3——问模型（2 次）：新句的\"更大的行\"半句让它把\"购置固定资产和无形资产\"当成比资本开支宽的一行，其中一次重想后改口\"就是资本开支\" | 照登记测全部 22 道题 |\n| 34 | 11-05 | 第三版的登记测试：和第 8 轮一样，只换这一句；读 132 次（0.078 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、读到数的 7 道题三遍重判、当天对照（108 次，0.268 美元） | **三条全过**：v10、v12 各 9/9；判对的题一道没变错（只有 v22 第 2 遍反过来对照 0/3、起点组 3/3，理由是在 41% 和 32% 两个增长估计间摇摆，和第 13 轮方向相反，与起点数无关）；46 个数全是自己的量（2 条是标明的二季度指引），两人分类全一致；v19、v20 的错数全没了，其余 15 道白卷、没冒出新错。没盖住：资本开支那一行试发时漏过 1 次；指引也被写成\"事实\"；一路判下来的效果和跨题效果要端到端才量得到 | 交 Alex：要不要做进生产、跑 14 天三遍端到端 |\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json; from pathlib import Path; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind import model; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); v7=p.FROZEN_SYSTEM.read_text(); print(\\\"canonical matches\\\",sum(model.request_identity(p.rebuild(r,v7))[1]==j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"] for j,r,_,_ in map(p.stored,cs)),len(cs)); print(\\\"raw orders\\\", {tuple(json.loads(b[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"content\\\"])) for _,_,b,_ in map(p.stored,cs)}); \nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in {\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\"}:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); print(c[\\\"case\\\"], j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"], list(json.loads(b[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"content\\\"])),a[\\\"rung\\\"], a[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json; from pathlib import Path; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind import model; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); v7=p.FROZEN_SYSTEM.read_text(); print(\\\"canonical matches\\\",sum(model.request_identity(p.rebuild(r,v7))[1]==j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"] for j,r,_,_ in map(p.stored,cs)),len(cs)); print(\\\"raw orders\\\", {tuple(json.loads(b[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"content\\\"])) for _,_,b,_ in map(p.stored,cs)}); \nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in {\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\"}:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); print(c[\\\"case\\\"], j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"], list(json.loads(b[\\\"choices\\\"][0][\\\"message\\\"][\\\"content\\\"])),a[\\\"rung\\\"], a[\\\"implied_outcome\\\"])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"canonical matches 48 48\nraw orders {('rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed', 'theme_pushes', 'reference', 'implied_outcome')}\nT-new-3-Q26 f53fec27713023f149c3d6985dc4b450ad29ff544784b7e863580f444e8e6b4f ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed', 'theme_pushes', 'reference', 'implied_outcome'] 2 About $24.9bn: $6bn Poolside licence + $1bn Poolside investment + $13bn Groq closing consideration + $4bn Groq payable + about $0.9bn Enfabrica.\nT-old-2-Q16 35db1c9f46def85eba90d2eee48b2a970e41617b7919d75882a41ced01bab5fd ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed', 'theme_pushes', 'reference', 'implied_outcome'] 1 Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: about $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales plus a roughly $5 billion Grace contribution, using Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the recent run-rate proxy.\nT-old-3-Q3 9014621fefb0b4ba151907ba77d9f16a80fdf13449960b6aecd0fd72f6428a9d ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed', 'theme_pushes', 'reference', 'implied_outcome'] 1 About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py | sed -n '1,260p'; nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '178,330p'; nl -ba src/hivemind/model.py | sed -n '109,146p;238,295p'; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10007,10027p;10102,10132p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py | sed -n '1,260p'; nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '178,330p'; nl -ba src/hivemind/model.py | sed -n '109,146p;238,295p'; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10007,10027p;10102,10132p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"D3, the judgement score (ruler-v1.md §1): for each gold question, the run's last judgement on or before the window end; a numeric\n     2\tquestion agrees when the gold's implied range overlaps the band of the rung the judge chose, a yes/no question when the answers match.\n     3\tPrints every question, the totals, the two null baselines (the rung containing the gold's reference figure; always the neutral rung) and\n     4\tthe gold's own reader-disagreement count. Refuses a gold whose sha256 or ruler version is not the one written here (D261).\n     5\t\n     6\t    python3 score_judgement.py <run folder> [--day 2026-07-14] [--map q23=v25 ...]\n     7\t\"\"\"\n     8\timport argparse\n     9\timport hashlib\n    10\timport json\n    11\timport sqlite3\n    12\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    13\t\n    14\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    15\tRULER_VERSION = 1\n    16\tGOLD_SHA256 = '636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2'   # frozen 2026-11-02 (D262); gold-v1.sha256 holds the same value\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\tdef main():\n    20\t    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    21\t    ap.add_argument('run'); ap.add_argument('--day', default='2026-07-14'); ap.add_argument('--map', nargs='*', default=[])\n    22\t    args = ap.parse_args()\n    23\t    gold_path = HERE / 'gold-v1.json'\n    24\t    sha = hashlib.sha256(gold_path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    25\t    gold = json.loads(gold_path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))\n    26\t    if gold.get('ruler_version') != RULER_VERSION:\n    27\t        raise SystemExit(f'gold ruler_version {gold.get(\"ruler_version\")} != scorer {RULER_VERSION}: refused')\n    28\t    frozen = (HERE / 'gold-v1.sha256').read_text().split()[0] if (HERE / 'gold-v1.sha256').exists() else None\n    29\t    if GOLD_SHA256 and frozen != GOLD_SHA256:\n    30\t        raise SystemExit(f'gold-v1.sha256 {frozen} != the frozen value in this scorer {GOLD_SHA256}: refused')\n    31\t    if frozen and frozen != sha:\n    32\t        raise SystemExit(f'gold sha256 {sha} != frozen {frozen}: refused')\n    33\t    corrections = json.loads((HERE / 'corrections-v1.json').read_text()) if (HERE / 'corrections-v1.json').exists() else []\n    34\t    label = 'FROZEN' if frozen else f'DRAFT, {gold.get(\"status\")}'\n    35\t    print(f'gold v{RULER_VERSION} sha256 {sha[:16]}… [{label}]; corrections on record, not applied: {len(corrections)}; run {args.run}; window end {args.day}')\n    36\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{args.run}/views.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True); conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n    37\t    mapping = {k: v for k, v in (m.split('=') for m in args.map)}\n    38\t    seeds = {f'q{k:02d}': f'v{k}' for k in range(1, 23)}\n    39\t    questions = {q['id']: q for q in json.loads((HERE / 'questions-v1.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))['questions']}\n    40\t    for qid, vid in seeds.items():     # the seeds keep their ids in every run: check the text, refuse a run that differs\n    41\t        row = conn.execute('select question from views where view_id=?', (vid,)).fetchone()\n    42\t        if not row or row['question'] != questions[qid]['question']:\n    43\t            raise SystemExit(f'{vid} in the run is not the seed question {qid}: refused')\n    44\t    mapping = {**seeds, **mapping}\n    45\t    if 'q23' not in mapping:\n    46\t        hit = conn.execute(\"select view_id from views where question like '%Ohio%' and question like '%guarantee%' order by created_seq limit 1\").fetchone()\n    47\t        if hit:\n    48\t            mapping['q23'] = hit['view_id']\n    49\t    totals = {'agree': 0, 'disagree': 0, 'no judgement': 0, 'not in run': 0, 'gold open': 0, 'no_view': 0}\n    50\t    base_lookup = [0, 0]; base_neutral = [0, 0]\n    51\t    print(f'{\"q\":<4}{\"outcome\":<14}{\"gold implied\":<28}{\"judge rung / band\":<40}{\"lookup\":<8}{\"neutral\":<8}question')\n    52\t    for entry in gold['entries']:\n    53\t        qid = entry['question_id']; q = questions[qid]\n    54\t        if entry.get('no_view'):\n    55\t            totals['no_view'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"no_view\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n    56\t        if not entry.get('implied_outcome'):\n    57\t            totals['gold open'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"gold open\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n    58\t        resolved = entry.get('resolution') or entry\n    59\t        implied, direction = resolved['implied_outcome'], resolved.get('direction')\n    60\t        vid = mapping.get(qid)\n    61\t        if not vid:\n    62\t            totals['not in run'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"not in run\":<14}{\"\":<28}{\"\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n    63\t        j = conn.execute('select rung from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1', (vid, args.day)).fetchone()\n    64\t        rungs = [dict(r) for r in conn.execute('select position, label, lo, hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position', (vid,))]\n    65\t        if j is None:\n    66\t            totals['no judgement'] += 1; print(f'{qid:<4}{\"no judgement\":<14}{str(implied):<28}{vid + \" never judged\":<40}{\"\":<8}{\"\":<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}'); continue\n    67\t        chosen = rungs[j['rung'] - 1]\n    68\t        if q['unit'] == 'yes/no':\n    69\t            agree = (chosen['label'].lower() == 'yes') == bool(implied['yes_no'])\n    70\t            gold_text = 'yes' if implied['yes_no'] else 'no'; band = chosen['label']\n    71\t            lookup = neutral = None\n    72\t        else:\n    73\t            lo, hi = chosen['lo'], chosen['hi']\n    74\t            agree = (lo is None or implied['high'] >= lo) and (hi is None or implied['low'] < hi)\n    75\t            gold_text = f'{implied[\"low\"]:,.4g}..{implied[\"high\"]:,.4g}'; band = f'{lo if lo is not None else \"-inf\"}..{hi if hi is not None else \"inf\"}'\n    76\t            ref = ((entry.get('reference') or {}).get('A') or {}).get('value') if isinstance(entry.get('reference'), dict) and 'A' in entry['reference'] else (entry.get('reference') or {}).get('value')\n    77\t            lookup = None\n    78\t            if ref is not None:\n    79\t                for r in rungs:\n    80\t                    if (r['lo'] is None or ref >= r['lo']) and (r['hi'] is None or ref < r['hi']):\n    81\t                        lookup = (r['lo'] is None or implied['high'] >= r['lo']) and (r['hi'] is None or implied['low'] < r['hi']); break\n    82\t            neutral = None\n    83\t            n = next((r for r in rungs if r['label'].lower() == 'neutral'), None)\n    84\t            if n:\n    85\t                neutral = (n['lo'] is None or implied['high'] >= n['lo']) and (n['hi'] is None or implied['low'] < n['hi'])\n    86\t        totals['agree' if agree else 'disagree'] += 1\n    87\t        if lookup is not None:\n    88\t            base_lookup[0] += lookup; base_lookup[1] += 1\n    89\t        if neutral is not None:\n    90\t            base_neutral[0] += neutral; base_neutral[1] += 1\n    91\t        print(f'{qid:<4}{(\"agree\" if agree else \"DISAGREE\"):<14}{gold_text:<28}{(vid + \" rung \" + str(j[\"rung\"]) + \" \" + chosen[\"label\"][:12] + \" \" + band)[:39]:<40}'\n    92\t              f'{(\"\" if lookup is None else (\"yes\" if lookup else \"no\")):<8}{(\"\" if neutral is None else (\"yes\" if neutral else \"no\")):<8}{q[\"question\"][:60]}')\n    93\t    judged = totals['agree'] + totals['disagree']\n    94\t    print(f'\\ntotals: {totals}; agreement {totals[\"agree\"]}/{judged} of judged questions')\n    95\t    print(f'null baselines: the rung containing the gold reference agrees {base_lookup[0]}/{base_lookup[1]}; always neutral agrees {base_neutral[0]}/{base_neutral[1]}')\n    96\t    noise = (gold.get('readers') or {}).get('stick_noise') or {}\n    97\t    print(f'gold = reader B (D262); control reader A differs on {len(noise.get(\"range_or_yesno_disagreements\", []))} questions by range or yes/no and on {len(noise.get(\"direction_label_splits\", []))} direction labels; [{label}]')\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   101\t    main()\n   178\tdef retry_note(errors):\n   179\t    return '\\n\\nThe code returned the previous answer because: ' + '; '.join(errors) + '\\nCorrect it and answer again, citing only the listed ids.'\n   180\t\n   181\t\n   182\tdef themes_shown_in(request_text):\n   183\t    \"\"\"The theme ids of the block a request carried: the ids the answer may name, whatever the ledger says now.\"\"\"\n   184\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Themes in force, ', 1)\n   185\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (v\\d+) \\(', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   186\t\n   187\t\n   188\tdef evidence_shown_in(request_text):\n   189\t    \"\"\"The evidence ids the request listed: the ids the answer may cite and push.\"\"\"\n   190\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Evidence in ', 1)\n   191\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (e\\d+) \\[', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   192\t\n   193\t\n   194\tdef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None):\n   195\t    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n   196\t    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now.\"\"\"\n   197\t    errors = []\n   198\t    shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n   199\t    named = []\n   200\t    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n   201\t        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n   202\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n   203\t        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n   204\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n   205\t        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n   206\t    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n   207\t    allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n   208\t    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n   209\t    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n   210\t    deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   211\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   212\t    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n   213\t        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n   214\t    for cited in data['cites']:\n   215\t        if cited not in allowed:\n   216\t            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n   217\t    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n   218\t        if cited not in deps:\n   219\t            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n   220\t    for push in data['pushes']:\n   221\t        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n   222\t            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n   223\t    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n   224\t        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n   225\t            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n   226\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   227\t    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n   228\t        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n   229\t    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n   230\t        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n   231\t    return errors\n   232\t\n   233\t\n   234\tdef _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n   235\t    data = vocabulary.judgement_answer(answer.data)\n   236\t    judgement_id = store.insert(conn, 'judgements', view_id=view_id, seq=seq, input_id=item['input_id'], rung=data['rung'],\n   237\t                                reasons=data['reasons'], cited_evidence_json=json.dumps(data['cites']), cited_views_json=json.dumps(data['cites_views']),\n   238\t                                pushes_json=json.dumps(data['pushes'], ensure_ascii=False), changed=1 if data['changed'] else 0,\n   239\t                                theme_pushes_json=json.dumps(data.get('theme_pushes', []), ensure_ascii=False),\n   240\t                                reference=data.get('reference'), implied_outcome=data.get('implied_outcome'),\n   241\t                                why_changed=data['why_changed'], config_checksum=cfg.checksum, call_id=answer.call_id,\n   242\t                                request_sha256=answer.request_sha256, publication_day=item['publication_day'])\n   243\t    conn.commit()\n   244\t    return {'view_id': view_id, 'judgement_id': judgement_id, 'rung': data['rung'], 'changed': data['changed'], 'cost': answer.cost_usd, 'cached': answer.cached}\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\tJUDGE_WORKERS = 32     # a day's round is up to 40 judgements of about 16 s each in two dependency levels (themes, then assets); 32 at a time makes a round about two calls long (2026-11-02)\n   248\t\n   249\t\n   250\tdef upstream(conn, view_id):\n   251\t    \"\"\"Every view this one depends on, directly or through its dependencies.\"\"\"\n   252\t    seen, stack = set(), [view_id]\n   253\t    while stack:\n   254\t        for dep in store.dependencies_of(conn, stack.pop()):\n   255\t            target = dep['resolved_to']\n   256\t            if target and target not in seen:\n   257\t                seen.add(target)\n   258\t                stack.append(target)\n   259\t    return seen\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\tdef open_asset_views(conn):\n   263\t    return [v for v in store.open_views(conn) if store.view(conn, v)['object_kind'] != 'theme']\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\tdef queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n   267\t    \"\"\"What a changed conclusion of this view queues: its dependants and, for a theme thesis, every open asset thesis. The one rule,\n   268\t    read for a live judgement and for the judgements a resumed round finds committed.\"\"\"\n   269\t    queued = store.dependants_of(conn, view_id)\n   270\t    if store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme':\n   271\t        queued = queued + open_asset_views(conn)\n   272\t    return queued\n   273\t\n   274\t\n   275\tdef queued_by_ledger(conn, seq):\n   276\t    \"\"\"What the judgements already committed at this sequence number queued; a fresh round finds nothing here, a resumed round\n   277\t    finds what the interrupted one had queued in memory.\"\"\"\n   278\t    queued = []\n   279\t    for row in conn.execute('select view_id from judgements where seq=? and changed=1 order by judgement_id', (seq,)):\n   280\t        queued.extend(queued_by_change(conn, row['view_id']))\n   281\t    return queued\n   282\t\n   283\t\n   284\tdef round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, changed_views, seq, item, *, transport=None):\n   285\t    \"\"\"Judge the open views in changed_views, dependencies first; a changed conclusion queues open dependants.\n   286\t\n   287\t    Views that do not depend on each other are judged at the same time: each level of the dependency order is sent\n   288\t    to the model concurrently (the prompts are built and the answers checked and written in this thread, so the\n   289\t    database connection never crosses threads). A view waits while anything upstream of it is still pending, so a\n   290\t    dependant always reads this round's conclusion of its dependency. A view with no evidence in force stays\n   291\t    unjudged (D59), and a view already judged at this sequence number (a resumed round) is not judged twice.\n   292\t\n   293\t    Among the views ready to be judged, theme theses go first, so an asset thesis reads this round's theme conclusions; a theme judgement that says changed queues every open asset thesis with\n   294\t    evidence in force. The starting queue adds what the ledger says earlier commits at this sequence number queued.\"\"\"\n   295\t    import concurrent.futures\n   296\t\n   297\t    version = cfg.rejudging.prompt_version\n   298\t    schema = schema_for(version)\n   299\t\n   300\t    def eligible(view_id):\n   301\t        return store.is_open(conn, view_id) and not store.has_judgement(conn, view_id, seq) and bool(store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id))\n   302\t\n   303\t    def is_theme(view_id):\n   304\t        return store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme'\n   305\t\n   306\t    pending = [v for v in topological(conn, list(changed_views) + queued_by_ledger(conn, seq)) if eligible(v)]\n   307\t    done, results = set(), []\n   308\t    while pending:\n   309\t        ready = [v for v in pending if upstream(conn, v).isdisjoint(set(pending) - {v})] or pending[:1]\n   310\t        level = [v for v in ready if is_theme(v)] or ready\n   311\t        prompts = {v: build_prompt(conn, v, version) for v in level}\n   312\t\n   313\t        def ask(view_id, note=''):\n   314\t            system, user = prompts[view_id]\n   315\t            return model.call(archive, cfg, cfg.rejudging, system, user + note, schema, 'judgement', 'judge', transport=transport)\n   316\t\n   317\t        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=JUDGE_WORKERS) as pool_:\n   318\t            answers = dict(zip(level, pool_.map(ask, level)))\n   319\t        for view_id in level:\n   320\t            answer = answers[view_id]\n   321\t            errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1])\n   322\t            if errors:\n   323\t                archive.retire(answer.call_id)                 # a refused answer never answers the same request again (a resume re-samples)\n   324\t                answer = ask(view_id, retry_note(errors))\n   325\t                errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1])\n   326\t                if errors:\n   327\t                    archive.retire(answer.call_id)\n   328\t                    raise JudgementRefused(f'{view_id}: ' + '; '.join(errors))\n   329\t            result = _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer)\n   330\t            done.add(view_id)\n   109\t    name = ENDPOINTS[provider][1]\n   110\t    if os.environ.get(name):\n   111\t        return os.environ[name]\n   112\t    with open(CREDENTIALS, encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   113\t        return json.load(handle)[name]\n   114\t\n   115\t\n   116\tdef build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider):\n   117\t    request = {\n   118\t        'model': setting.model,\n   119\t        'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': system}, {'role': 'user', 'content': user}],\n   120\t        'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'name': schema_name, 'strict': True, 'schema': schema}},\n   121\t    }\n   122\t    if provider == 'openai':\n   123\t        request['max_completion_tokens'] = setting.max_output_tokens\n   124\t        if setting.reasoning_effort:\n   125\t            request['reasoning_effort'] = setting.reasoning_effort\n   126\t    else:\n   127\t        request['max_tokens'] = setting.max_output_tokens\n   128\t    return request\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\tdef openai_transport(provider, request, timeout=180, max_attempts=3):   # the default preserves the production retry behavior\n   132\t    url, _ = ENDPOINTS[provider]\n   133\t    body = json.dumps(request).encode('utf-8')\n   134\t    headers = {'Authorization': f'Bearer {_key(provider)}', 'Content-Type': 'application/json'}\n   135\t    last = None\n   136\t    last_http = None\n   137\t    if isinstance(max_attempts, bool) or not isinstance(max_attempts, int) or max_attempts < 1:\n   138\t        raise ValueError('max_attempts must be a positive integer')\n   139\t    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n   140\t        try:\n   141\t            with urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, headers=headers, method='POST'), timeout=timeout) as response:\n   142\t                raw_bytes = response.read()\n   143\t                try:\n   144\t                    raw = raw_bytes.decode('utf-8')\n   145\t                except UnicodeDecodeError:\n   146\t                    if max_attempts != 1:\n   238\t    if kind == 'boolean':\n   239\t        return [] if isinstance(value, bool) else [f'{path}: expected boolean']\n   240\t    if kind == 'null':\n   241\t        return [] if value is None else [f'{path}: expected null']\n   242\t    return []\n   243\t\n   244\t\n   245\tdef request_identity(request):\n   246\t    \"\"\"The exact serialized request and its archive key, shared by callers and validation.\"\"\"\n   247\t    encoded = json.dumps(request, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False)\n   248\t    return encoded, hashlib.sha256(encoded.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n   249\t\n   250\t\n   251\tdef call(archive, cfg, setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, purpose, *, transport=None):\n   252\t    provider = cfg.provider\n   253\t    request = build_request(setting, system, user, schema, schema_name, provider)\n   254\t    request_json, sha = request_identity(request)\n   255\t    hit = archive.lookup(sha)\n   256\t    prompt_chars = len(system) + len(user)\n   257\t    if hit:\n   258\t        call_id, answer_json, _, input_tokens, output_tokens = hit\n   259\t        return Answer(json.loads(answer_json), call_id, sha, True, prompt_chars, input_tokens or 0, output_tokens or 0, 0.0)\n   260\t    started = time.time()\n   261\t    response = (transport or openai_transport)(provider, request)\n   262\t    seconds = round(time.time() - started, 1)\n   263\t    try:\n   264\t        content = response['choices'][0]['message']['content']\n   265\t        data = json.loads(content)\n   266\t    except (KeyError, IndexError, TypeError, json.JSONDecodeError) as error:\n   267\t        archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n   268\t                       request_sha256=sha + ':malformed:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n   269\t                       response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=None, prompt_chars=prompt_chars,\n   270\t                       input_tokens=0, output_tokens=0, cost_usd=0.0, seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n   271\t        raise SchemaError(f'malformed answer: {error}')\n   272\t    errors = check_schema(data, schema)\n   273\t    usage = response.get('usage') or {}\n   274\t    input_tokens = int(usage.get('prompt_tokens') or 0)\n   275\t    output_tokens = int(usage.get('completion_tokens') or 0)\n   276\t    cached_tokens = min(input_tokens, int((usage.get('prompt_tokens_details') or {}).get('cached_tokens') or 0))\n   277\t    price_in, price_out = PRICES.get(setting.model, (0.0, 0.0))\n   278\t    cost = round((input_tokens - cached_tokens) / 1e6 * price_in + cached_tokens / 1e6 * price_in * CACHED_INPUT_SHARE + output_tokens / 1e6 * price_out, 6)\n   279\t    call_id = archive.record(purpose=purpose, config_version=cfg.version, config_checksum=cfg.checksum, model=setting.model,\n   280\t                             request_sha256=sha if not errors else sha + ':schema:' + uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], request_json=request_json,\n   281\t                             response_json=json.dumps(response)[:200000], answer_json=json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False),\n   282\t                             prompt_chars=prompt_chars, input_tokens=input_tokens, output_tokens=output_tokens, cost_usd=cost,\n   283\t                             seconds=seconds, created_at=time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'))\n   284\t    if errors:\n   285\t        raise SchemaError('; '.join(errors[:5]))\n   286\t    return Answer(data, call_id, sha, False, prompt_chars, input_tokens, output_tokens, cost)\n 10007\t## D676 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison: the new system loses for two known mechanisms; keep the old system; the outcome was computable before the run (council consensus)\n 10008\t\n 10009\t- **Alex:** \"did you measure wrong or what … you go deep into the entire trace and logic and run council loop to reason on this\n 10010\t  comparison and the runs of the two system\".\n 10011\t- **Council:**\n 10012\t  - an independent cold Codex read (`docs/research/2026-11-19-comparison-review/council-a-codex.md`);\n 10013\t  - Claude's reflection, written first (`claude-reflection.md`);\n 10014\t  - one challenge round (`council-b-codex.md`).\n 10015\t- **Agreed:**\n 10016\t  - **The measurement is sound for the products as configured.**\n 10017\t    - The matter list was built blind from the articles.\n 10018\t    - The mapping was blind with third readers.\n 10019\t    - The controls pass, 13 of 13.\n 10020\t    - The old system's served cards hold on the ones read.\n 10021\t    - No new question was mapped out wrongly.\n 10022\t    - Codex found the old system's Anthropic serve generous; without it b is 6 or 7, still a loss.\n 10023\t  - **Mechanism 1, by design (D382, D384, D505):** the company's own figures are not questions. 7 of the 32 matters are such figures,\n 10024\t    and the old system serves 4 to 6 of them. By D384, five other companies' standalone figures are also outside the new system's\n 10025\t    rules, so its ceiling is 20 of 32 matters.\n 10026\t  - **Mechanism 2, a hand-off defect known since D432:**\n 10027\t    - Step 1 opens bundled questions (two years, or several outcomes), and the frozen ladder refuses them. new-2 lost 27 of 60 and new-3\n 10102\t## D680 · 2026-11-19 · Merge test closed (council e): delivery verified, the registered test did not pass (P2's proxy clause and V missed)\n 10103\t\n 10104\t- **Ran under D679**, for $2.13 of a $5 ledger: 140 c8 sends, 98 judge calls in two arms, 15 noise re-sends, and two blind reader\n 10105\t  families on 20 cards with 5 controls.\n 10106\t- **Held:**\n 10107\t  - P1, delivery, 4/4: the hybrid's final requests carry c8's four-company $720–745B item and the Reuters anchor-talks items;\n 10108\t  - P3, talks stay talks, 2/2;\n 10109\t  - P5, both readers support all 4 hybrid answers, settled in round 0;\n 10110\t  - P4 (diagnostic): 0 NVIDIA-figure proposals of 7;\n 10111\t  - the reader controls: 3 plants unsupported, 2 controls supported.\n 10112\t- **Missed:**\n 10113\t  - **P2's second clause.** Total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy; old misses it the same way.\n 10114\t  - **V.** The control's v47 was unsupported. Its 09-16 request, re-sent, gave rungs 2, 1, 2 against old's 2, 2, 2. The requests differ in\n 10115\t    the theme block and the previous wording, so a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n 10116\t- **What the answers rest on.** Capex rests partly on 09-06 history: 26–27 c8 items against 16–20 history items in the final requests.\n 10117\t  The Anthropic answers rest wholly on c8's filing.\n 10118\t- **Errors of mine** (caught by Codex):\n 10119\t  - the census numbering misread;\n 10120\t  - a first article list with off-matter theses;\n 10121\t  - a judging copy refused by the store's append-only triggers.\n 10122\t- **Closed with no further sends.** The next step is Alex's.\n 10123\t- Record: `docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md`, `council-e-codex.md`.\n 10124\t\n 10125\t## D681 · 2026-11-20 · Alex opens the hybrid system's build and test; phase 1 is a new judge version (J2) for the judge's reasoning errors\n 10126\t\n 10127\t- Alex, 2026-11-20, after the merge test (D680) and the finding that the old system loses on the 32 matters at opening and in the judge's\n 10128\t  reasoning, not in reading: \"不是聊了半天就是要合并两边最好的来提升系统吗？\"; then, on the proposal to fix the judge's reasoning errors\n 10129\t  first as a new judge version, with a council review and a test of a few cents: \"可以啊，你现在开始做hybrid system 的构建和测试，不要犯了和做新系统一样的错误到最后才发现问题\".\n 10130\t- Decided (his words): the hybrid system is built and tested. The judge (judging-v7, frozen by D354) may get a new version, J2, measured\n 10131\t  against v7. The frozen ruler is unchanged.\n 10132\t- The plan (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md`) guards against the new system's mistakes:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,re; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); terms={\\\"T-new-2-Q20\\\":\\\"30%|225 billion|400 billion\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q26\\\":\\\"Texas|108.5 billion\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q27\\\":\\\"tariff|Not shown\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q5\\\":\\\"midplane|2026-07-05|roadmap is intact\\\", \\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\":\\\"e2470 |Enfabrica|closing consideration|investment in Groq\\\", \\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\":\\\"total CPU|total server CPU|standalone CPU|trailing 12\\\", \\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\":\\\"e4510 |e5192 |20%|12% sequential\\\", \\\"T-old-3-Q41\\\":\\\"full production|first Vera|shipping|double\\\"};\nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in terms:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); u=r[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"].splitlines(); print(c[\\\"case\\\"],j[\\\"publication_day\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\"); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in u if re.search(terms[c[\\\"case\\\"]],l,re.I))[:4000])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,re; from pathlib import Path; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind import model; scorer=Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold\\\"); seeds={f\\\"q{i:02d}\\\":f\\\"v{i}\\\" for i in range(1,23)}; gold={e[\\\"question_id\\\"]:e for e in json.loads((scorer/\\\"gold-v1.json\\\").read_text())[\\\"entries\\\"]}; bas=Path(\\\"poc/data/e2e-r9-frozen-v108-2026-11-03\\\"); frozen=p.FROZEN_SYSTEM.read_text();\nfor d in sorted(bas.glob(\\\"d*\\\")):\n c=p.readonly(d/\\\"views.sqlite\\\"); a=p.readonly(d/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\"); ohio=c.execute(\\\"select view_id from views where lower(question) like ? and lower(question) like ? order by created_seq limit 1\\\",(\\\"%ohio%\\\",\\\"%guarantee%\\\")).fetchone(); counts={}; dates={};\n for q,v in (seeds | ({\\\"q23\\\":ohio[0]} if ohio else {})).items():\n  j=c.execute(\\\"select * from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1\\\",(v,\\\"2026-07-14\\\")).fetchone()\n  if not j: continue\n  dates[j[\\\"publication_day\\\"]]=dates.get(j[\\\"publication_day\\\"],0)+1; call=a.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where request_sha256=?\\\",(j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"],)).fetchone(); k=(q==\\\"q23\\\",bool(gold[q].get(\\\"no_view\\\")),bool(call and model.request_identity(p.rebuild(json.loads(call[0]),frozen))[1]==j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"])); counts[k]=counts.get(k,0)+1\n print(d.name,dict(sorted(dates.items())),counts,\\\"Ohio\\\",tuple(ohio) if ohio else None)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,re; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); terms={\\\"T-new-2-Q20\\\":\\\"30%|225 billion|400 billion\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q26\\\":\\\"Texas|108.5 billion\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q27\\\":\\\"tariff|Not shown\\\", \\\"T-new-2-Q5\\\":\\\"midplane|2026-07-05|roadmap is intact\\\", \\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\":\\\"e2470 |Enfabrica|closing consideration|investment in Groq\\\", \\\"T-old-2-Q16\\\":\\\"total CPU|total server CPU|standalone CPU|trailing 12\\\", \\\"T-old-3-Q3\\\":\\\"e4510 |e5192 |20%|12% sequential\\\", \\\"T-old-3-Q41\\\":\\\"full production|first Vera|shipping|double\\\"};\nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in terms:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); u=r[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"].splitlines(); print(c[\\\"case\\\"],j[\\\"publication_day\\\"],sep=\\\" | \\\"); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(l for l in u if re.search(terms[c[\\\"case\\\"]],l,re.I))[:4000])'\"","aggregated_output":"T-new-2-Q20 | 2026-09-15\nThesis v32 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"AI-infrastructure debt's share of net new investment-grade bond issuance in calendar 2026 (the item reports as much as 30% in some indexes) and whether that issuance continues to clear against multi-decade-high sovereign yields and 6%-plus long-duration corporate yields.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What percentage of net new investment-grade bond issuance in calendar 2026 will AI-infrastructure borrowers represent, as reported in index-provider full-year new-issue composition statistics and issuance tallies?\n- e2318 [rumour; Investing.com (via Yahoo Finance), citing Reuters; original source Private credit market commentary cited by Reuters ; from gnews:cceb2198c53ad33ba3328321 2026-08-19] AI-infrastructure debt now accounts for as much as 30% of net new issuance in some investment-grade indexes this year, according to private credit market commentary cited by Reuters.\n    Quote: AI infrastructure debt now accounts for as much as 30% of net new issuance in some investment-grade indexes this year, according to private credit market commentary cited by Reuters.\n- e2360 [third_party_view; The Verge; original source S&P Global ; from gnews:9d8a9b8bf88a2be11484a45e 2026-08-19] S&P Global data cited by the article show hyperscalers and related companies such as Nvidia had issued about $225 billion in bonds as of the end of July, with almost 10-fold growth in this segment's bond issuance through midyear, and S&P said it could issue $400 billion by the end of the year.\n    Quote: As of the end of July, the hyperscalers “and related companies” like Nvidia had issued about $225 billion in bonds, according to S&P Global. “This segment has seen almost 10-fold growth in its bond issuance through midyear,” S&P noted, and could issue $400 billion by the end of the year.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is the reported “as much as 30%” in some investment-grade indexes; 30% is in rung 3, but it is neither a full-market figure nor a full-year result. The broader S&P segment tally and year-end projection still imply roughly 28%, within rung 3. NVIDIA’s confirmed $25.0 billion issue supports continued issuance, but it is not added separately to S&P’s broader tally and its stated general-corporate-purpose use does not establish that the full amount is AI-infrastructure borrowing. Higher yields and pressure on large buyers’ cash generation weigh against further acceleration, but no quantified revision, cancellation, or delay shifts the implied share across a rung boundary.\nT-new-2-Q26 | 2026-09-20\n- e3000 [fact; Fortune; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:4f0b142ac84dc52deff2898a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA disclosed maximum gross guarantee exposure of $108.5 billion, mostly from credit support for SB Energy's Ohio tech campus that will host NVIDIA compute leased to OpenAI; the remaining $3.5 billion backs lease obligations for AI cloud partners.\n    Quote: Still, Nvidia’s involvement has been critical and substantial and, moreover, has led to a drag on the stock price. The company disclosed maximum gross guarantee exposure of $108.5 billion, which mostly stems from credit support for SB Energy’s Ohio tech campus, which will host Nvidia compute leased to OpenAI. The rest is due to $3.5 billion backing lease obligations for AI cloud partners.\n- e3041 [fact; Saxo; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:94a91c45c7f3cfd7ebb5e84b 2026-08-26] NVIDIA disclosed maximum gross guarantees of $108.5 billion, including as much as $105 billion connected with the OpenAI and SB Energy data-centre development in Ohio.\n    Quote: maximum gross guarantees of $108.5 billion , including as much as $105 billion connected with the OpenAI and SB Energy data-centre development in Ohio;\n- e3091 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68d0c633478c534c9766b00e 2026-08-27] NVIDIA disclosed as much as $108.5 billion of guarantees, almost all of it — $105 billion — tied to the SB Energy data center build-out for OpenAI; the support is not paid up front but phases in as nine data centers come online beginning around fiscal 2029 and shrinks as OpenAI makes payments.\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed as much as $108.5 billion of guarantees. Almost all of that — $105 billion — is tied to the SB Energy data center build-out for OpenAI . … That support is not money Nvidia is handing over today. It phases in as nine data centers come online beginning around fiscal 2029 and shrinks as OpenAI ( OPAI.PVT ) makes payments.\n- e3291 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com (Georgia Butler); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9d1e3eb66e02fc0326d93f74 2026-08-28] In its Q2 earnings NVIDIA disclosed $108.5 billion in guarantees and other agreements to enable AI cloud computing, of which $3.5bn was for land, power and shell guarantees for AI clouds and $105bn was toward SB Energy Corp for OpenAI's Ohio campus.\n    Quote: Notably, the earnings also showed that Nvidia disclosed $108.5 billion in guarantees and other agreements to enable AI cloud computing. $3.5bn was for land, power, and shell guarantees for AI clouds, while $105bn was toward SB Energy Corp for OpenAI's Ohio campus.\n- e3382 [fact; Fortune (Jim Edwards), citing NVIDIA's Q2 10-Q; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4c18bb0cd3382da09ddeea86 2026-08-28] In the same 10-Q, NVIDIA disclosed $56 billion of AI-cloud and third-party lease commitments, $108.5 billion of land and power guarantees, and memorandums of understanding with various finance providers covering more than $500 billion of third-party capital over time.\n    Quote: On top of that, Nvidia disclosed $56 billion in AI-cloud and third-party lease commitments, land and power guarantees of $108.5 billion, and “memorandums of understanding” with various finance providers for “more than $500 billion of third-party capital over time,” per the 10-Q.\n- e3746 [fact; finance.yahoo.com, citing a Wall Street Journal report; original source Anthropic (with Lambda, NVIDIA and Hut 8) ; from gnews:d2d1a1c0a0520d170a8efdcb 2026-09-01] Anthropic signed a $35 billion cloud-compute contract, backed by NVIDIA, under which Anthropic will rent GPU compute from Lambda — an NVIDIA-backed cloud provider — while NVIDIA supplies the chips and holds the lease on the underlying data center, a 700-megawatt campus in Nueces County, Texas being developed by Hut 8. The figure is a contracted multi-year commitment, not reported revenue; the item says NVIDIA plays three roles in one transaction (chip supplier, equity backer of Lambda, lease anchor), having signed its own \nT-new-2-Q27 | 2026-08-27\nThesis v42 (us-china-tariffs, theme, focus \"A formal U.S. tariff action on semiconductors (presidential proclamation / Federal Register notice, e.g. under Section 232 or IEEPA) or an explicit administration decision not to impose such duties, and the scope of any duties.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will the Trump administration issue a formal action imposing new U.S. tariffs on semiconductors by January 31, 2027, as verified by a presidential proclamation or an official Federal Register or Commerce notice and the resulting tariff schedule?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: After August 27, 2026, and by January 31, 2027, the Trump administration issues a presidential proclamation or an official Federal Register or Commerce notice imposing new U.S. tariffs on semiconductors. / rung 2 Ruled out: After August 27, 2026, and by January 31, 2027, the Trump administration formally decides not to impose the contemplated new semiconductor tariffs or formally abandons that tariff initiative as a whole. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-08-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n- e3136 [rumour; CNBC (citing an unnamed report; White House official quoted); original source  2026-08-27; from gnews:a625887f511b79f425e27824 2026-08-27] A report emerging on Thursday [2026-08-27] said U.S. President Donald Trump's administration is considering new tariffs on U.S. semiconductors; a White House official told CNBC that, unless officially announced by the Administration, any reporting about tariffs should be regarded as baseless speculation.\n    Quote: Both Beijing and Washington have looked to leverage political heft to gain an advantage, with a report emerging on Thursday that U.S. President Donald Trump's administration is considering new tariffs on U.S. semiconductors. … A White House official told CNBC that \"unless officially announced by the Administration, any reporting about tariffs should be regarded as baseless speculation.\"\nT-new-2-Q5 | 2026-09-20\nThesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"First commercial shipments of Kyber (or confirmation of a slip to 2028) and resolution of the PCB midplane manufacturability issue.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture reach first commercial shipments in calendar 2027, as confirmed by NVIDIA shipment or production disclosures by December 31, 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA confirms by December 31, 2027 that the first commercial shipments of Kyber NVL144 occurred during calendar 2027; a production announcement without confirmation of commercial shipments does not suffice. / rung 2 Ruled out: After July 5, 2026, NVIDIA formally cancels Kyber NVL144 or decides to defer its first commercial shipments until after December 31, 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n- e99 [third_party_view; CNBC (citing SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post on Monday that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 (from a planned 2027 debut with Vera Rubin Ultra) because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects electronic modules remains challenging to manufacture; it also said the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked by optical connections) is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\n    Quote: The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday. … \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\n- e100 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack architecture is delayed, stating \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n- e101 [third_party_view; CNBC (citing SemiAnalysis); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis reported that a backup plan to bolt two of NVIDIA's current-generation racks together to achieve comparable scale-up for Rubin Ultra was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with \"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\" — which the firm said could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google, whose in-house chips already win business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\n- e126 [third_party_view; datacenterdynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA has delayed production of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028 from an originally planned 2027, because the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.\n- e130 [party_statement; datacenterdynamics (Charlotte Trueman); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-06; from gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d 2026-07-06] In response to a request for comment from DataCenterDynamics about the Kyber delay claims, an NVIDIA spokesperson said: 'Our roadmap is intact.'\n    Quote: In response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e112 (); both are in f\nT-new-3-Q26 | 2026-09-20\nThesis v37 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed individual and cumulative amounts NVIDIA pays or commits under its licence-and-hire structures (Poolside licence fee and equity stake, Groq, Enfabrica), and confirmation that the Poolside licence and $1bn investment close on the announced terms.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What cumulative amount, in USD, has NVIDIA paid or committed under the Poolside Model Factory licence and associated equity investment, Groq arrangement and Enfabrica arrangement as of FY2027 year-end, counting paid amounts only once, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K and intervening Form 10-Q filings?\n- e2470 [fact; thenextweb.com (TNW), citing The Decoder for Enfabrica; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7c42792d6f3e1fbdc877492e 2026-08-21] TNW reports that across three licence-and-hire deals — Poolside ($6bn licence plus $1bn investment), Groq ($20bn for its inference technology and top engineers, paid in December) and the hardware startup Enfabrica (about $900m, according to The Decoder) — NVIDIA has committed roughly $27bn. Each was structured as a licence plus a hiring round rather than an outright purchase, and in each case the target company continued to operate independently.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's reported aggregate of roughly $27bn, which sits in rung 3. The disclosed component amounts imply about $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus the announced $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. That is below rung 3's $25bn boundary. Groq's $2.944bn cash outflow and $986m accrued consideration are payment details within the Groq arrangement, not additional amounts to add; the $2.5bn intangible asset is also not additional consideration. No evidence shown indicates a further commitment that would lift the total across $25bn by FY2027 year-end.\nT-old-2-Q16 | 2026-09-20\nThesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $20 billion and at most $25 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total CPU revenue is less than $10 billion.\n- e896 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.\n    Quote: Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,” adding: “We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.”\n- e897 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales.\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang: “The $20 billion is for standalone CPU.”\n    Quote: Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\n- e2544 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal '28\n- e2735 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Vera CPU was in full production and expected total CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028, citing customer demand and an improving supply outlook.\n    Quote: In its fiscal Q2 2027 report, Nvidia revealed that its Grace family of CPUs had already generated $5 billion in sales over the trailing 12-month period\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales; that reference is rung 3. The most likely total is just over $25 billion: the more specific FY2027 Vera statements put Vera CPU sales at about $20 billion, while Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027. The CFO’s broader “total CPU revenue” wording makes the addition less certain, but the separate Vera and Grace figures support treating Grace as additional rather than replacing the Vera outlook. Wolfe’s 2026 shipment estimate implies only about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, and AMD’s performance claims add execution risk; neither provides a quantified revision to the FY2027 outlook or enough to move the central estimate below \nT-old-3-Q3 | 2026-09-20\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion (110% of the $392 billion analyst estimate). / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion (within 10% below the analyst estimate). / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion (more than 20% below the analyst estimate).\n- e60 [third_party_view; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis’s projection; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue for the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\n- e4190 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied 12% sequential total revenue growth at the midpoint.\n    Quote: In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\n- e4510 [party_statement; Jon Quast; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] NVIDIA guided to 70% revenue growth for the coming year, relative to the revenue it is reporting this year.\n- e5134 [party_statement; zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management projected fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\n    Quote: Management projects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\n- e5192 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:ea4633ed3e4ad9d71383673f 2026-09-17] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue growth of 90% in FY2027 Q3 and 65% in FY2027 Q4.\n- e5369 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, or 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues to reach $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The market reference is about $411 billion (e5406), which is rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate remains above the rung-1 boundary: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion (e2754); applying the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance to the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 (e2715, e5088, e5089); carrying the 12% sequential growth pace forward implies about $127 billion for Q4 (e4190). That gives roughly $418 billion before SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift to second-half data-center compute, which adds about $27–28 billion when applied only to that portion, for an implied total near $446 billion (e60)—above $431.2 billion. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026’s $215.9 billion, a bearish counter-signal, but it is l\nT-old-3-Q41 | 2026-09-20\n- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which implies more than $40 billion and sits at rung 2. I discount that forecast because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand; the new AMD performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk, while broad deployment expectations and market-growth estimates support demand but do not quantify NVIDIA’s share. I therefore imply roughly $38–39 billion, below the $40 billion boundary and within rung 3.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cases=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); rungs={c[\\\"case\\\"]:p.stored(c)[0][\\\"rung\\\"] for c in cases}; out=collections.defaultdict(list);\nfor f in (p.OUT/\\\"probe/v7\\\").glob(\\\"*.json\\\"):\n d=json.loads(f.read_text()); name=d[\\\"case\\\"][\\\"case\\\"]; out[name].append(d[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]\"'!=rungs[name]);\nfor role in \"TC\":\n a=[r for name,r in out.items() if name.startswith(role) and len(r)==3]; print(role,\"completed\",len(a),\"draw flips\",sum(map(sum,a)),\"cases 2+\",sum(sum(r)>=2 for r in a),\"trials\",sum(len(x) for name,x in out.items() if name.startswith(role))); print(sorted((name,sum(r),len(r)) for name,r in out.items() if name.startswith(role) and sum(r)))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cases=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()); rungs={c[\\\"case\\\"]:p.stored(c)[0][\\\"rung\\\"] for c in cases}; out=collections.defaultdict(list);\nfor f in (p.OUT/\\\"probe/v7\\\").glob(\\\"*.json\\\"):\n d=json.loads(f.read_text()); name=d[\\\"case\\\"][\\\"case\\\"]; out[name].append(d[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]\"'!=rungs[name]);\nfor role in \"TC\":\n a=[r for name,r in out.items() if name.startswith(role) and len(r)==3]; print(role,\"completed\",len(a),\"draw flips\",sum(map(sum,a)),\"cases 2+\",sum(sum(r)>=2 for r in a),\"trials\",sum(len(x) for name,x in out.items() if name.startswith(role))); print(sorted((name,sum(r),len(r)) for name,r in out.items() if name.startswith(role) and sum(r)))'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"T completed 8 draw flips 3 cases 2+ 1 trials 24\n[('T-new-2-Q31', 3, 3)]\nC completed 7 draw flips 0 cases 2+ 0 trials 23\n[]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,re; from pathlib import Path; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; from hivemind import model; scorer=Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold\\\"); seeds={f\\\"q{i:02d}\\\":f\\\"v{i}\\\" for i in range(1,23)}; gold={e[\\\"question_id\\\"]:e for e in json.loads((scorer/\\\"gold-v1.json\\\").read_text())[\\\"entries\\\"]}; bas=Path(\\\"poc/data/e2e-r9-frozen-v108-2026-11-03\\\"); frozen=p.FROZEN_SYSTEM.read_text();\nfor d in sorted(bas.glob(\\\"d*\\\")):\n c=p.readonly(d/\\\"views.sqlite\\\"); a=p.readonly(d/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\"); ohio=c.execute(\\\"select view_id from views where lower(question) like ? and lower(question) like ? order by created_seq limit 1\\\",(\\\"%ohio%\\\",\\\"%guarantee%\\\")).fetchone(); counts={}; dates={};\n for q,v in (seeds | ({\\\"q23\\\":ohio[0]} if ohio else {})).items():\n  j=c.execute(\\\"select * from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1\\\",(v,\\\"2026-07-14\\\")).fetchone()\n  if not j: continue\n  dates[j[\\\"publication_day\\\"]]=dates.get(j[\\\"publication_day\\\"],0)+1; call=a.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where request_sha256=?\\\",(j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"],)).fetchone(); k=(q==\\\"q23\\\",bool(gold[q].get(\\\"no_view\\\")),bool(call and model.request_identity(p.rebuild(json.loads(call[0]),frozen))[1]==j[\\\"request_sha256\\\"])); counts[k]=counts.get(k,0)+1\n print(d.name,dict(sorted(dates.items())),counts,\\\"Ohio\\\",tuple(ohio) if ohio else None)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"d1 {'2026-07-14': 17} {(False, False, True): 17} Ohio None\nd2 {'2026-07-14': 17} {(False, False, True): 17} Ohio None\nd3 {'2026-07-13': 1, '2026-07-14': 16} {(False, False, True): 17} Ohio None\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text());\nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in {\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q37\\\"}:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); print(c[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"cites\\\"],a[\\\"reasons\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text());\nfor c in cs:\n if c[\\\"case\\\"] in {\\\"T-new-3-Q26\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q37\\\"}:\n  j,r,b,a=p.stored(c); print(c[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",r[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",a[\\\"cites\\\"],a[\\\"reasons\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"T-new-3-Q26 \n Thesis v37 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed individual and cumulative amounts NVIDIA pays or commits under its licence-and-hire structures (Poolside licence fee and equity stake, Groq, Enfabrica), and confirmation that the Poolside licence and $1bn investment close on the announced terms.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What cumulative amount, in USD, has NVIDIA paid or committed under the Poolside Model Factory licence and associated equity investment, Groq arrangement and Enfabrica arrangement as of FY2027 year-end, counting paid amounts only once, as disclosed in its FY2027 Form 10-K and intervening Form 10-Q filings?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 20,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 20,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (the amount last reported when the question opened: \"Across the three deals, Nvidia has committed roughly $27bn.\") / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 35,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 35,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 14 items]\n- e2469 [fact; thenextweb.com (TNW), citing Newcomer; original source Poolside (investor letter setting out the deal terms) 2026-08-21; from gnews:7c42792d6f3e1fbdc877492e 2026-08-21] NVIDIA has agreed to pay the AI startup Poolside $6bn for a non-exclusive licence of the software Poolside used to build AI models (its 'Model Factory'), to offer jobs to 109 of Poolside's staff, and to invest a further $1bn in what remains of the company at a $12bn pre-money valuation, according to an investor letter Poolside sent its shareholders that Newcomer first reported. The three Poolside founders are staying, the letter states the deal is 'not an acquisition and it is not an acquihire', and Poolside intends to distribute the $6bn to its investors by the end of next year. NVIDIA has not said what makes the licence worth $6bn.\n    Quote: Nvidia has agreed to pay Poolside $6bn to license the software the startup used to build AI models, and to offer jobs to 109 of its staff. It is also investing $1bn in what remains, at a $12bn pre-money valuation. … The terms come from a letter Poolside sent its investors, first reported by Newcomer . The licence is non-exclusive. The three founders are staying. … The deal is “not an acquisition a\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2470 [fact; thenextweb.com (TNW), citing The Decoder for Enfabrica; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7c42792d6f3e1fbdc877492e 2026-08-21] TNW reports that across three licence-and-hire deals — Poolside ($6bn licence plus $1bn investment), Groq ($20bn for its inference technology and top engineers, paid in December) and the hardware startup Enfabrica (about $900m, according to The Decoder) — NVIDIA has committed roughly $27bn. Each was structured as a licence plus a hiring round rather than an outright purchase, and in each case the target company continued to operate independently.\n    Quote: Across the three deals, Nvidia has committed roughly $27bn. Each was structured as a licence and a hiring round rather than a purchase, and in each case the company continued to operate. … TNW reported the template in May, when Nvidia paid Groq $20bn for its inference technology and took its top engineers. Groq stayed independent and raised $650m for what was left. … Nvidia struck a similar arrang\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3700, e4219; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2255 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Groq ; from gnews:f7751a62e0c6a2e9c6c7752c 2026-08-17] Groq shifted from being a pure AI chipmaker building its own LPUs to compete with Nvidia on inference into a cloud and data-centre provider that operates Nvidia systems, after Nvidia hired founder and CEO Jonathan Ross and other top talent in a $20 billion licensing deal last September; the pivot was kicked off with a $650 million round in June.\n    Quote: Groq was focused on building its own chips, dubbed LPUs (language processing units), to compete with Nvidia on inference — the type of compute needed to run AI workloads in real time. After it lost its star team, Groq shifted from being a pure AI chipmaker into a cloud and data center provider that operates Nvidia systems. … In June, Groq raised a $650 million round to kick off its pivot.\n- e2651 [fact; nationalcioreview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6699a9ad4297e55ba12b4817 2026-08-24] Nvidia announced a new deal with startup Poolside: it will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology and invest another $1 billion in Poolside at a $12 billion pre-money valuation. The non-exclusive licence covers Poolside’s Model Factory system, and the licensing payment is expected to go to Poolside investors before the end of next year. More than 100 Poolside engineers are joining Nvidia to work on its Nemotron open-weight models, while Poolside remains independent and its founders stay with the startup.\n    Quote: Nvidia is making a major investment in open-weight AI through a new deal with startup Poolside. The company will pay $6 billion to license Poolside’s AI technology and invest another $1 billion in the startup at a $12 billion pre-money valuation. … The deal also brings more than 100 Poolside engineers to Nvidia to work on Nemotron , its family of open-weight models. Nvidia plans to use Poolside’s \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2809 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 condensed consolidated cash flow statement shows a $2.944 billion financing cash outflow labeled \"Groq, Inc.\"\n    Quote: Groq, Inc.\n(2,944\n)\n-\n(2,944\n)\n-\n- e2894 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA disclosed $986 million of accrued purchase consideration as of July 26, 2026 related to its non-exclusive license agreement with Groq, Inc.\n    Quote: Accrued purchase consideration (3)\n986\n2,479 … (3) Related to the Groq, Inc. non-exclusive license agreement.\n- e3502 [rumour; tspasemiconductor.substack.com (SEMIVISION); original source tspasemiconductor.substack.com (SEMIVISION) ; from gnews:e02d9195cb9bb0a245bd15a0 2026-08-29] Nvidia has reportedly agreed to pay $6bn for a non-exclusive licence to Poolside's Model Factory, invest a further $1bn in Poolside and offer jobs to 109 of its employees, with Poolside surviving as a company, per the item.\n    Quote: The company has reportedly agreed to pay $6bn for a non-exclusive licence to Poolside’s Model Factory, invest another $1bn in the firm and offer jobs to 109 employees.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3700 [fact; BGR (Joel Loynds); original source Nvidia / Groq ; from gnews:0826f5551beb1e171f153a3e 2026-08-31] BGR reports that Nvidia's $20 billion deal with AI chip startup Groq was, technically, a licensing deal rather than an acquisition, and says some observers had misread it as Nvidia buying Groq out.\n    Quote: For instance, a $20 billion deal with Groq was misinterpreted by some as Nvidia buying them out, when it's — technically — a licensing deal.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2470; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3701 [fact; BGR (Joel Loynds); original source Nvidia / Poolside ; from gnews:0826f5551beb1e171f153a3e 2026-08-31] BGR reports that Nvidia's most recent licence-style deal is with startup Poolside, which is licensing out its AI models to Nvidia; the outlet says some reports frame the arrangement as an attempt by Nvidia to avoid merger scrutiny.\n    Quote: Most recently, it's done this with startup Poolside, which is licensing out its AI models to Nvidia, which some reports position as the company attempting to avoid merger scrutiny.\n- e3933 [fact; The Wall Street Journal; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:482b3fc6d69e13a8f37603c6 2026-09-03] The Wall Street Journal reported last month that Nvidia struck a $6 billion deal with coding startup Poolside to develop open models.\n    Quote: Last month, The Wall Street Journal reported that it struck a $6 billion deal with coding startup Poolside to develop open models.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4219 [rumour; Daniel Sparks (fool.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:a68f4be941102b46a8fbce0f 2026-09-06] Nvidia's December 2025 Groq deal was reportedly valued at about $20 billion and was a technology license and hiring arrangement, not a purchase of the company.\n    Quote: (Nvidia's December 2025 Groq deal was bigger, reportedly valued at about $20 billion, but that was a technology license and hiring, not a purchase of the company.)\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2470; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4663 [fact; TechTarget; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:04c2471fa33743b56d7cce76 2026-09-11] Nvidia disclosed the accounting for its Groq arrangement in regulatory filings: it said it did not purchase Groq's customer contracts, existing products or equity interests, and reported $13 billion in cash consideration at closing with another $4 billion payable within a year; it also recorded a $2.5 billion developed-technology intangible asset related to the arrangement.\n    Quote: Nvidia disclosed the financial and accounting details in regulatory filings. It said it didn't purchase Groq's customer contracts, existing products or equity interests. Nvidia reported $13 billion in cash consideration at closing, with another $4 billion payable within a year. … Nvidia also recorded a $2.5 billion developed-technology intangible asset related to the arrangement.\n- e4850 [fact; Crunchbase News; original source NVIDIA 2026-09-14; from gnews:069dd81a68850f1e6923d3f7 2026-09-14] Crunchbase reported that NVIDIA sharply accelerated its startup investing in August 2026: it participated in nine disclosed rounds of at least $5 million — its busiest month for investing since at least the beginning of 2025 — and led or co-led financings with an aggregate value of $1.3 billion. Seven of the nine qualifying investments were in companies Crunchbase categorized as AI-focused, including River AI, Poolside, Groq, Starcloud and Generalist AI. NVIDIA had participated in only four U.S. rounds of $5 million or more in July 2026 and one in August 2025. By mid-August 2026, it had participated in a record 59 known startup funding rounds in 2026, surpassing its 53 investments in all of 2025, and had led or co-led at least 11 private-company financings that year. Crunchbase describes the dollar figures as an approximation of spending, not a tally of capital actually contributed.\n    Quote: Chip giant Nvidia , meanwhile, sharply accelerated its dealmaking, ranking among the most active and highest-spending investors for the month. The chip giant participated in nine disclosed rounds of at least $5 million — marking its busiest month for investing since at least the beginning of 2025 — and led or co-led financings collectively valued at $1.3 billion. … Nvidia’s rise in the investor ra\n- e4851 [fact; Crunchbase News; original source Poolside / NVIDIA 2026-09-14; from gnews:069dd81a68850f1e6923d3f7 2026-09-14] Crunchbase reported that Poolside's $1 billion financing was one of the rounds making up NVIDIA's $1.3 billion in led or co-led August 2026 financings. The item does not state how much of that $1 billion NVIDIA itself contributed.\n    Quote: Nvidia and Sequoia came next, each with $1.3 billion in led or co-led rounds. Nvidia’s total came from Poolside’s $1 billion financing and Volta’s $300 million Series A, while Sequoia led or co-led four rounds, topped by the Valar Atomics financing. … As always, this is an approximation of spending rather than a tally of capital actually contributed. Investors rarely disclose how much each partici\n\n[Themes in force, 11]\n- v10 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What will the spot rental price in USD per H100 GPU-hour be on December 31, 2026, as reported in Ornn's compute-marketplace spot-price data?: now rung 4 from 3 to below 4; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the no-reference baseline: rung 3 ($2 to below $3). The latest direct Ornn reading is $3.28, already above that boundary; its reported 22% month-over-month rise, together with tight capacity and high utilization, makes a further but more moderate increase by December more likely tha\n- v14 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act (RASA) be enacted into US law by January 31, 2027, as recorded on Congress.gov?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-08-26; reasons: The reference is the reported legislative status: RASA passed the House but had not passed the Senate, which sits at rung 3, Still open. The evidence gives reasons for action—the reported remote-access loophole and Commerce Department review—but no Senate passage, enactment, or formal decision again\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan officially adopt performance-threshold export controls covering advanced AI chips, including Nvidia-class accelerators, destined for China by January 31, 2027, as established by Ministry of Economic Affairs commodity-list amendments or official Ministry of Economic Affairs or Executive Yuan announcements?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: No market consensus is supplied; the reference is rung 3, because the official position shown is consideration of controls, not a decision to adopt or reject the specified performance-threshold controls. The newer report of consideration of a criminal ban on all AI-chip exports to China strengthens \n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the US Department of Commerce/BIS publicly resolve its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced US AI chips by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end, January 31, 2027, as evidenced by a Commerce/BIS announcement, Federal Register rule, Entity List addition, or official no-action conclusion resolving that investigation?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The available reference is a formal Commerce/BIS investigation and review with no announced outcome or decision date, which sits at rung 3, Still open. Allegations and quantified reports of Chinese access to restricted chips increase the prospect of a response, and experts say BIS could issue a rule\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's government permit imports of Nvidia H200 AI chips into China by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end on January 31, 2027, as established by Chinese government, regulatory or customs announcements?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-20; reasons: The market/news reference sits at rung 1: reports describe limited permissions, reported official exceptions and some deliveries. But the question requires a Chinese government, regulatory or customs announcement establishing permission. The reports of officials telling companies they may buy limite\n- v30 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will combined calendar-2026 data-centre capital expenditure be for CoreWeave and Nebius, the two named neoclouds used as the fixed reporting cohort, as reported in their full-year earnings releases and annual filings?: now rung 4 from 60,000,000,000 USD to below 70,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is company guidance: CoreWeave's $35–39 billion plus Nebius's $20–25 billion gives $55–64 billion, with a $59.5 billion midpoint in rung 3 and the range extending into rung 4. The implied outcome is about $64 billion: the latest $9.4 billion CoreWeave quarterly capex report is consiste\n- v33 (ai-capex, due 2027-12-31) Will FERC publish a final disposition of its transmission-grid integration reforms for large loads such as AI data centres and their supporting co-located generation, through a final order or rule or a formal decision not to adopt reforms, by December 31, 2027?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The reference is FERC's stated process: operator and planner responses are due in late November 2026, and a real plan may not be finalized until 2027 at the earliest. That reference sits at rung 3, Still open: it sets no deadline for a final FERC disposition by December 31, 2027. The new evidence sh\n- v35 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What is total AI-buildout bond issuance, in USD, by hyperscalers and related companies including Nvidia during calendar 2026, as reported in S&P Global's full-year AI-related bond-issuance tracker, cross-checked against issuers' filings and debt-offering disclosures?: now rung 3 from 375,000,000,000 USD to below 425,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-15; reasons: The reference is S&P Global's projection of up to about $400 billion for calendar 2026, which is in rung 3. Extending the reported roughly $225 billion through end-July at the same average monthly pace implies about $386 billion for the year, also in rung 3. NVIDIA's $25 billion June offering is alr\n- v44 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much total global venture capital funding will AI companies raise in calendar 2026, in USD, as reported in PitchBook's year-end global AI venture-funding data?: now rung 4 from 400,000,000,000 USD to below 650,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The reference is $265 billion raised in all of 2025, which is rung 3; no separate 2026 consensus or guidance is supplied. The >$400 billion H1 claim points to a much higher full-year total if carried forward, but August global venture funding of $42 billion implies about $252 billion of all-market f\n- v55 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How many GW of combined new AI factory capacity will NVIDIA's eight Australian DSX partners—Firmus, Sharon AI, IREN, Megaport, ResetData, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk—have brought online in Australia by 31 December 2027, as established by their capacity disclosures and Data Centres Australia's capacity statistics?: now rung 2 from 0.5 GW to below 1.8 GW; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's target of up to 2GW by 2027, which sits in rung 3 (1.8GW to below 2.2GW). The evidence supports a forecast of about 0.9GW, not the full target: I credit the 42MW Firmus facility and some, but not all, of CDC's 800MW under construction and IREN's disclosed development. IREN'\n- v57 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) How many desktop discrete graphics cards (add-in boards) will the industry ship in calendar 2026, as reported in Jon Peddie Research's full-year 2026 desktop AIB shipment report and quarterly updates?: now rung 3 from 46,000,000 count to below 50,000,000 count; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The supplied reference has no consensus or guidance figure; the ladder’s no-reference anchor is rung 3 (46–50 million). The evidence implies roughly 49 million: the 24.3 million H1 total is a 48.6 million annual pace, while Q2’s 12.5 million shipments and sequential growth support a modest upward ad\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's reported aggregate of roughly $27bn, which sits in rung 3. The disclosed component amounts imply about $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus the announced $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn cash consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. That is below rung 3's $25bn boundary. Groq's $2.944bn cash outflow and $986m accrued consideration are payment details within the Groq arrangement, not additional amounts to add; the $2.5bn intangible asset is also not additional consideration. No evidence shown indicates a further commitment that would lift the total across $25bn by FY2027 year-end.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n ['e2469', 'e2470', 'e2809', 'e2894', 'e4663', 'e4851'] The reference is NVIDIA's reported aggregate of roughly $27bn, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about $24.9bn: Poolside's $6bn licence plus $1bn investment, Groq's $13bn closing consideration plus $4bn payable, and about $0.9bn for Enfabrica. The Groq cash outflow and accrued consideration are payment details within that arrangement, not additional amounts; the $2.5bn intangible asset is not additional consideration. The implied total remains below the $25bn rung-3 boundary.\nT-old-3-Q37 \n Thesis v37 (NVDA, asset, focus \"FY2028 gross margin\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 gross margin is greater than 73%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 gross margin is greater than 72% and no greater than 73%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and no greater than 72%. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 gross margin is at least 70% and less than 71%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 gross margin is less than 70%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 30 items]\n[Not shown: 13 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e2788 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA forecast Q3 FY2027 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 73.5%–74.5%, Q4 FY2027 margins of 71%–72%, and FY2028 margins of 72%–73%.\n    Quote: For Q3, we expect GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to be 74% plus or minus 50 basis points. We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2717, e2863, e2885, e2969, e2977, e3003, e3228, e3312, e3374, e3426, e3839, e3870, e4308; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2789 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said memory price increases had exceeded its prior expectations and were expected to rise further in FY2028.\n    Quote: The magnitude of the price increase has exceeded our prior expectations and are headed even higher into next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2970, e3019, e3068; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2978 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com, quoting Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:81226ce463750079ebf335c5 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said significantly higher component costs and extreme memory pricing led NVIDIA to reset its gross-margin expectations.\n    Quote: Many of you have expressed concerns regarding our gross margins as component costs have risen significantly. As you are already aware, we are experiencing extreme pricing conditions in memory... As a result, we are resetting expectations today,\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2970, e3986, e5161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3193 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source OpenAI (processor unveiling); analysts quoted by CNBC (margin assessment) 2026-08-27; from gnews:2544f0dce0571202a4bcbc2e 2026-08-27] OpenAI unveiled Jalapeño, its first custom inference processor developed with Broadcom, which CNBC-reported analysts said poses a threat to NVIDIA's inference margins.\n    Quote: The same day The Information released its report about the Nvidia-Hugging Face deal, OpenAI unveiled Jalapeño, its first custom inference processor developed with Broadcom; analysts told CNBC it poses a threat to Nvidia's margins in inference, a.k.a. the business of actually running trained models, which is where AI spending is growing the fastest right now.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2982; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3227 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by Fox Business; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:b9e66d60b2cd9c574fd4cb5c 2026-08-27] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had repriced its products in the marketplace.\n    Quote: We have also repriced our products in the marketplace\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3221; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3228 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by Fox Business; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:b9e66d60b2cd9c574fd4cb5c 2026-08-27] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said NVIDIA’s gross margins would fall from 75% to 72%–73% next fiscal year, FY2028.\n    Quote: our margins are going to come down from 75%. But it will be between 72% and 73% next year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2788, e4122; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3378 [third_party_view; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source fool.com (Anders Bylund) 2026-08-28; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] The author estimated that high-bandwidth memory now represents 30%–40% of an AI accelerator’s build cost, up from under 20% two generations ago.\n    Quote: High bandwidth memory has grown to roughly 30% to 40% of an AI accelerator's build cost, up from under 20% two generations ago.\n- e3379 [third_party_view; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source fool.com (Anders Bylund) 2026-08-28; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] The author said Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron are the only three manufacturers of high-bandwidth memory.\n    Quote: Exactly three companies on earth make the stuff: Samsung ( SSNLF +0.00% ) , SK Hynix ( SKHY +4.29% ) , and Micron ( MU +4.29% ) .\n- e3380 [fact; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source Micron ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] Micron is adding manufacturing lines in Idaho, Virginia, and New York over the next eight years.\n    Quote: Micron is adding manufacturing lines in Idaho, Virginia, and New York over the next eight years.\n- e3381 [party_statement; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] SK Hynix plans to open four memory facilities in South Korea in 2029.\n    Quote: Hynix plans to open four Korean memory facilities in 2029.\n- e3382 [fact; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source Samsung ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] Samsung moved the planned opening of its next factory forward from 2031 to 2029.\n    Quote: Samsung accelerated the opening of its next factory from 2031 to 2029.\n- e3383 [fact; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] NVIDIA signed a multiyear memory partnership with SK Hynix during the quarter.\n    Quote: Nvidia also signed a multiyear memory partnership with SK Hynix during the quarter.\n- e3384 [party_statement; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said FY2028 gross-margin recovery would occur as executed price increases take effect in Q1 FY2028.\n    Quote: Kress said the fiscal 2028 recovery will happen \"as executed price increases take effect in Q1\" of that fiscal year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2979, e4123; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e3385 [party_statement; fool.com (Anders Bylund); original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:92e19c04dbca319f84450451 2026-08-28] Colette Kress said NVIDIA’s price increases had already been negotiated.\n    Quote: The increases are already negotiated.\n- e3987 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-01; from gnews:ee0002bd6bda4aa8d9a483f5 2026-09-01] The author says AI infrastructure demand is driving a memory shortage and higher memory prices, hurting NVIDIA and its peers.\n    Quote: The colossal levels of demand for AI infrastructure -- something that's clearly benefiting Nvidia -- are also driving the memory shortage and memory price increase -- something that's hurting Nvidia and peers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3021; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4154 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler (fool.com); original source SK Hynix; Micron and its rivals ; from gnews:621e1b678a037049c86a70af 2026-09-04] SK Hynix predicted DRAM supply would remain constrained through at least 2030; Micron and its rivals had begun locking in contracts of up to five years.\n    Quote: Rival SK Hynix has predicted that the DRAM market will remain supply-constrained through at least 2030, while Micron and its rivals have begun to lock in long-term contracts for up to five years for the first time ever.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4925; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4251 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8653711909bcf63268b8735b 2026-09-05] NVIDIA management expects gross margin of 71%–72% in FY2027 Q4 and 72%–73% in FY2028, citing higher memory prices.\n    Quote: But management expects margins of 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter and 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028, due to pressure from higher memory prices.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3020, e4282, e4760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4282 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:49351bd6de4706de524baf5f 2026-09-05] NVIDIA expects gross margin to be about 71%–72% in FY2027 Q4 and 72%–73% in FY2028, attributing much of the pressure to sharp memory-price increases.\n    Quote: Nvidia expects its gross margins to fall from 75% in the fiscal second quarter to around 71% to 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter, before improving to around 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028. Management attributed much of this pressure to sharp increases in memory prices.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4251; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4308 [third_party_view; calcalistech.com; original source calcalistech.com 2026-09-06; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] The article expected NVIDIA FY2028 gross margin to stabilize at about 72%–73%.\n    Quote: In fiscal 2028, the gross margin is expected to stabilize at around 72%-73%, after the price increases take effect in the first quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2788; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4388 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:595a83074fb8af0237f822ce 2026-09-06] NVIDIA expects gross margin to decline from 75% in Q2 FY2027 to 71%–72% in Q4 FY2027, then reach 72%–73% in FY2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia expects gross margins to fall from 75% in the fiscal second quarter to around 71% to 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter, before improving to around 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2979, e5152, e5156, e5182; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4916 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:66141c741456bbfa753600f6 2026-09-13] NVIDIA management expected gross margin of 72% to 73% in FY2028.\n    Quote: and settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3374, e5197; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5016 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] NVIDIA expects non-GAAP gross margin of 71%-72% in Q4 FY2027, recovering to 72%-73% in FY2028.\n    Quote: It expects non-GAAP gross margin to fall toward 71%-72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 before recovering to 72%-73% in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2979; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5146 [third_party_view; Zacks Equity Research; original source Gartner ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] Gartner expects memory supply to remain tight in 2027 as AI infrastructure increases consumption, potentially constraining supply for other applications.\n    Quote: Gartner expects memory supply to remain tight in 2027 as AI infrastructure continues to increase memory consumption, potentially limiting the pace of growth in automotive, industrial and other non-AI applications.\n- e5152 [third_party_view; Zacks Equity Research; original source Zacks Equity Research 2026-09-16; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] Zacks projects NVIDIA gross margin to fall from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 71%–72% in fiscal Q4 2027, then recover to 72%–73% in FY2028 as price increases take effect.\n    Quote: On the downside, rising memory prices are expected to weigh on NVIDIA's profitability, with gross margin projected to decline from 75% in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 to 71%-72% in the fourth quarter before recovering to 72%-73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4388; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5156 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] NVIDIA reported a 75% gross margin in fiscal Q2 2027, guided to 71%–72% in fiscal Q4 2027, and guided to 72%–73% for fiscal 2028.\n    Quote: Gross margin was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management now guides it to a bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027 before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4388; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5181 [party_statement; NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, quoted by fool.com; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said memory price increases had exceeded expectations and were expected to rise further into the next fiscal year.\n    Quote: The magnitude of the price increase has exceeded our prior expectations and are headed even higher into next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3922; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5182 [third_party_view; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] NVIDIA expects gross margin to decline over the next couple of quarters, then improve slightly in the next fiscal year, driven by higher chip prices.\n    Quote: Nvidia expects its gross margin to drop over the next couple of quarters. It expects a slight improvement in the next fiscal year, driven by higher chip prices.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4388; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5183 [third_party_view; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] SK Hynix estimates memory demand could exceed supply beyond 2030, despite adding capacity.\n    Quote: The company estimates that memory demand could outstrip supply beyond 2030, even as it brings more capacity online.\n- e5184 [third_party_view; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source KB Securities ; from gnews:4fbadb3b4cb5846297a34b0a 2026-09-16] KB Securities estimates SK Hynix holds about 10 days of memory inventory.\n    Quote: Korean investment firm KB Securities estimates that SK Hynix is sitting on memory inventory of just 10 days.\n- e5197 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] NVIDIA guided for gross margin of 72%–73% in FY2028.\n    Quote: The company also guided for gross margins of between 72% and 73% in its fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4916; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 10]\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence favors rung 1 because reports describe approvals for selected companies and official except\n- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%: its floor, 72%, is rung 3, while outcomes above 72% through 73% are rung 2. The implied forecast remains about 72.0%: negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery, but worsening memory costs and unusually tight supply make the guidance floor more likely than a result above it. The new supply evidence strengthens that pressure but gives no quantified NVIDIA-specific cost impact or guidance cut, so it does not support a forecast below 72% or move the outcome across a rung boundary.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n ['e2788', 'e3384', 'e3385', 'e5181', 'e5183', 'e5184'] The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%: exactly 72% is rung 3, while outcomes above 72% through 73% are rung 2 (the range midpoint, 72.5%, is in rung 2). I forecast about 72.0%, at the guidance floor: negotiated price increases taking effect in Q1 support recovery, but further memory-price increases and tight supply make the floor more likely than a result above 72%. That is rung 3, not below it; the new supply details strengthen the downside pressure but do not quantify a NVIDIA-specific cost impact or revise its FY2028 guidance enough to imply a different rung.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
